Stage 1 — Understand the basic chatbot
         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^   
          First we'll build:
                              Client
                                 │
                                 ▼
                              Chat API
                                 │
                                 ▼
                              LLM
                                 │
                                 ▼
                              Response

Example:

POST :  /api/v1/chat
Request:
          {
            "conversationId": "123",
            "message": "What is the capital of France?"
          }
Response: 
          {
            "conversationId": "123",
            "message": "The capital of France is Paris."
          }

This teaches us the fundamental request/response flow.
====================================================================================================================================
Stage 2 — Introduce conversation memory

A real chatbot needs context.
Suppose the user says:

                    User:
                    What is the capital of France?
                    
                    Bot:
                    Paris.
                    
                    User:
                    What is its population?

The second question is meaningless to the LLM unless we provide conversation context.

So we'll introduce:
             ┌───────────────┐
             │ Conversation  │
             │   Service     │
             └───────┬───────┘
                     │
                     ▼
               Conversation DB

Conversation:
              conversationId = abc123
              
              USER:
              What is the capital of France?
              
              ASSISTANT:
              Paris.
              
              USER:
              What is its population?

The orchestration layer constructs the LLM request:

              System prompt
              +
              Conversation history
              +
              Current user message
====================================================================================================================================
Stage 3 — Make it generic
          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

        This is the most important architectural step.
        
        Instead of writing: BankingChatbotService
        we create: ChatbotService and define a generic contract.
        
        For example:        
                    public interface Chatbot {
                    
                        ChatResponse chat(ChatRequest request);
                    }
        
        Then:
                  Chatbot
                     │
         ┌───────────┼────────────┐
         │           │            │
     GenericBot   BankingBot   InsuranceBot

      But even this isn't ideal.
      
      We don't want to create a new Java class for every customer.
      
      Instead, we'll make the chatbot configuration-driven.
    Something like:
              {
                "botId": "banking-assistant",
                "model": "some-llm",
                "systemPrompt": "...",
                "knowledgeBase": "banking-documents",
                "tools": [
                  "getAccountBalance",
                  "getTransactions"
                ]
              }

      Now the platform dynamically creates the chatbot behavior.
      
      That is what makes it truly reusable.
====================================================================================================================================
Stage 4 — Add RAG

      Now the chatbot becomes useful for enterprise applications.
      
      Suppose an insurance company has:
                  1000 PDF documents
                  500 policy documents
                  claim guidelines
                  customer manuals
                  regulations
                  FAQ documents

We don't want to send all of these documents to the LLM.

Instead:
              Documents
                 │
                 ▼
              Document ingestion
                 │
                 ▼
              Chunking
                 │
                 ▼
              Embeddings
                 │
                 ▼
              Vector Database

At runtime:
            User question
                  │
                  ▼
            Create embedding
                  │
                  ▼
            Vector search
                  │
                  ▼
            Relevant documents
                  │
                  ▼
                  LLM
                  │
                  ▼
                Answer



This is Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG).

  Architecture:
    
                         ┌───────────────┐
